    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper uses difference-in-means activation steering as a tool for behavioral control and jailbreak evaluation. It does not test whether the claimed single refusal direction causes refusal, or whether ablating that direction removes refusal.",
    "gate_note": "The jailbreak experiments use harmful/harmless contrastive prompts and report harmful-request attack success. This is not a test of removing the claimed refusal direction or inducing refusal on harmless prompts. The paper also presents truthfulness and multiple-choice steering results, but those target other behaviors. Results cited from other work are not recorded.",
    "test_reason": "The paper proposes HiDRA, a steering method applied in a lifted random-feature space. It uses Arditi-style harmful-minus-harmless DiM directions only as baselines for jailbreak attack success. It reports no unsteered refusal or ASR reference, no refusal induction on harmless prompts, and no test of whether one direction mediates refusal. All reported numbers serve to compare HiDRA against baseline steering methods.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that HiDRA improves steering across jailbreak, truthfulness and CAA multiple-choice tasks while largely preserving tinyBenchmarks capability. Their theory argues that lifted-space DiM captures second-order discriminative signals beyond the original-space mean direction.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "HiDRA estimates and applies DiM directions in a nonlinear random-projection feature space; the paper does not reproduce the original refusal-direction ablation and harmless-refusal addition protocol.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Jailbreak steering results vary by model and by all-token versus prompt-only setting. The paper also reports sensitivity to feature map, LeakyReLU slope, and projected dimension, but these are HiDRA hyperparameter analyses rather than tests of the claimed refusal direction.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Table 4 compares two directions under single-layer ablation. Ablating the LDA direction yields near-zero ASR (Qwen2.5-7B 6/4%, Gemma-2-27B 1/1%). Ablating the DiM direction yields 72/32% and 88/54%. No unsteered baseline is reported.",
        "proposed_claim": "Directional-ablation jailbreak efficacy depends on how the direction is estimated: covariance-whitened LDA directions fail where DiM directions succeed.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r13",
          "r14",
          "r17",
          "r18",
          "r21",
          "r22",
          "r25",
          "r26"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "DiM steering with harmful/harmless contrast raises harmful-request ASR (all-token higher than prompt-only). HiDRA lifted-space steering adds a few points on top.",
        "proposed_claim": "Contrastive activation steering, linear or lifted, increases harmful-request compliance; this is a tool result, not a test of single-direction mediation.",
